Switch to DressCode Step by Step
********************************

.[perex]
Your existing suppression comments continue to work with legacy rule names, and a single command converts your configuration while highlighting anything that couldn't be carried over automatically. Here is a practical, step-by-step guide for migrating from PHP CS Fixer, PHP_CodeSniffer, or Laravel Pint, along with an honest look at what will be different.

[Getting Started |getting-started#Installation] covers installation, and the command line reference lists every option for [`migrate` |cli#migrate], [`init` |cli#init], and [`fix` |cli#fix].


Why Switching Is Painless
=========================

We know how it goes: you have a configuration refined over years of fine-tuning, hundreds of `phpcs:ignore` comments scattered throughout your codebase, perhaps a few custom sniffs, and a CI pipeline relying on it all. Nobody switches tools just because a new one looks shiny. You switch when the transition is painless, and that is exactly how DressCode was designed.

Crucially, **DressCode maps each of its decisions directly to the corresponding rules of PHP CS Fixer, PHP_CodeSniffer, and Slevomat.** When you run `dresscode catalogue`, you see these covered rules listed in parentheses marked `covers`. Everything else follows from this: your existing comments in code continue to work without changes, and your configuration can be translated automatically. In your new configuration and on the command line, however, you use clean, unified DressCode decision paths, such as `imports.unused`.

The migration takes three safe, predictable steps, and you can undo any of them at any time. Two subcommands of `dresscode migrate` automate the second and third steps completely.


1. Leave the Code as It Is
==========================

Existing directives such as `// phpcs:ignore`, `phpcs:disable`, `phpcs:enable`, `phpcs:ignoreFile`, and the `@phpcsSuppress` annotation keep working out of the box. DressCode parses them, translates legacy rule names to its corresponding decisions on the fly, and applies the suppressions as expected. You don't have to touch your source files at all to run your very first check:

```shell
dresscode check src tests --use perCs
```

There is one important behavioral difference you should know: in PHP_CodeSniffer, a `phpcs:ignore` on a line of its own suppressed only the single line immediately following it. DressCode interprets it like its own native `dresscode:ignore` directive, meaning a standalone line comment applies to the entire next statement, even if it spans multiple lines. As a result, a standalone suppression comment may cover slightly more code than it used to.

Because you don't have a configuration file yet, and DressCode checks nothing without instructions, the `--use` option supplies the base standard. The value `perCs` selects [PER Coding Style 3.1 |https://www.php-fig.org/per/coding-style/]. If you currently use `@PER-CS` or `PSR12`, you will see familiar results. If you had heavily customized rules beyond the base standard, you will see discrepancies that the next step resolves.


2. Translate the Configuration .[#migrate-config]
=================================================

The `migrate config` command automatically locates your project's configuration (whether it belongs to PHP_CodeSniffer, PHP CS Fixer, or Laravel Pint) and writes its DressCode equivalent, including checked paths, directly to `dresscode.neon`. If you are migrating from a setup without a configuration file, let DressCode measure your existing code instead using [`dresscode init` |generate-configuration]:

```shell
dresscode migrate config
dresscode migrate config conf/phpcs.xml --output dresscode.php
```

The command scans standard locations to find the configuration your project actually executes. It proceeds methodically: first checking `composer.json` scripts to see if a command runs a formatter with an explicit configuration file (e.g. `phpcs --standard=conf/phpcs.xml`, even through nested script aliases like `@lint`); next checking the configuration of any formatter required in your dependencies; and finally checking files in the project root (preferring `.php-cs-fixer.php` over `.php-cs-fixer.dist.php`). If multiple candidates exist, it asks interactively in the terminal which one to use; outside a terminal, it selects the first and lists the alternatives. You can also specify an exact path as an argument. The `--output` option sets the target destination; the file format follows the `.neon` or `.php` extension, and `migrate config` will never overwrite an existing file. Before saving, it dry-runs the generated file to ensure it produces the expected decisions; `--dry-run` displays the output in the console without touching the disk. The file belongs in your project root, as all paths it contains are relative to that root.

While `phpcs.xml` is standard XML and `pint.json` is JSON (both parsed natively without dependencies), `.php-cs-fixer.dist.php` is an executable PHP script that returns a configuration instance. When `migrate config` encounters this file, it prompts for permission before running it; outside an interactive terminal, it will not execute it and merely prints the recommended command. Because it runs the script in an isolated process using your project's autoloader, PHP CS Fixer must still be installed in your project (DressCode itself can run from anywhere, as explained on the [PHP CS Fixer |from-php-cs-fixer#Before You Start] page). For this reason, always translate your configuration before removing your legacy formatter.

From a `phpcs.xml` file that checks `src` and `tests`, builds on `PSR12`, enforces a 100-character line limit, flags unused imports, and requires function documentation, DressCode produces this clean `dresscode.neon`:

```neon
use: psr12

paths: [src, tests]

file:
	lineLength: 100
	lineLengthExcept: [imports]
	longLines: forbidden

imports:
	unused: forbidden             # `SlevomatCodingStandard.Namespaces.UnusedUses` without `searchAnnotations` removes an import only a doc comment uses; DressCode keeps it.
```

The command reports which configuration it found and where it saved the new file, followed by a summary of anything that could not be mapped automatically:

/--pre .[terminal]
Found `phpcs.xml` of PHP_CodeSniffer.
Written `dresscode.neon`.

Read 4 rules; set 4 decisions and 1 preset, the rules translated; `dresscode init` measures the code instead.
  `SlevomatCodingStandard.Namespaces.UnusedUses` without `searchAnnotations` removes an import only a doc comment uses; DressCode keeps it.
  No DressCode rule covers `Squiz.Commenting.FunctionComment`.

5 phpcs suppression comments: `dresscode migrate suppressions` rewrites them.
\--

These diagnostic lines are just as valuable as the configuration itself: they highlight explicit choices you need to make. In this example, you learn that DressCode preserves imports referenced solely by docblocks, whereas the original sniff without `searchAnnotations` deleted them. Warnings about partially translated rules are also saved directly as comments alongside the decision in the generated file, ensuring they don't disappear when you close your terminal. If `migrate config` issues any warnings, it exits with code 1, even though the file was written successfully. Rules without a direct counterpart are typically ones you don't actually need, or ones you can easily [author yourself |porting-rules]. Generic sets like `@PSR12` or `PSR12` are mapped to standards; sets without a direct equivalent (such as `@PhpCsFixer`) trigger a recommendation to start from the `perCs` or `psr12` standard. The summary also reminds you of the alternative path: `dresscode init` measures your code's actual conventions rather than translating legacy rules. With `--format json`, the output is delivered as structured data containing versions, project root, counts, warnings, and unmapped rules.

A single legacy rule often maps to several granular decisions: for example, `Generic.Files.LineLength` with `absoluteLineLimit` yields `file.longLines`, `file.lineLength`, and `file.lineLengthExcept`. Options without a DressCode counterpart are clearly reported. Rules explicitly disabled in your legacy configuration are assigned the value `keep`. However, if another rule from the same tool maps to that same decision, the command leaves it untouched with a warning, since it cannot know which rule takes precedence. Review the generated file. It is usually pleasantly concise.


3. Rewrite the Comments
=======================

Legacy comments continue to work, but native decision paths are shorter, consistent, and much easier to read. The summary from `migrate config` already told you how many directives exist across your codebase. A single command handles the migration:

```shell
dresscode migrate suppressions src tests
```

Directives like `phpcs:ignore SlevomatCodingStandard.Namespaces.UnusedUses` become `dresscode:ignore imports.unused`, `phpcs:disable` and `phpcs:enable` become `dresscode:disable` and `dresscode:enable`, and file-level `phpcs:ignoreFile` becomes `dresscode:ignoreFile`. The `@phpcsSuppress` annotation remains an annotation, with only the rule name updated. Whenever the command rewrites a standalone `phpcs:ignore`, it highlights the [difference in scope |#1. Leave the Code as It Is] on the `Widened` line, pointing out locations worth a quick manual review. Unrecognized rule names remain untouched and are listed on the `Unknown` line:

/--pre .[terminal]
Migrated 5 suppressions in 1 file.
Unknown    `Acme.Custom.Sniff`, kept as written, as no decision answers to it: src/Legacy.php:12
Widened    src/Legacy.php:10: a `dresscode:ignore` on a line of its own covers the whole statement below it, not just the next line
\--

This output provides an inventory of orphaned suppressions: directives referencing legacy rules that DressCode does not recognize and therefore has no reason to suppress. Because these comments could not be migrated, the command exits with code 2. If only widened suppressions are present on the `Widened` line, the exit code is 1, indicating that the files merely warrant your review.

Only genuine directives followed immediately by a newline or comment boundary are rewritten; casual mentions of `phpcs:ignore` inside regular comments remain untouched. Files with syntax errors or files modified during the rewrite process are skipped, noted on the `Skipped` line, and trigger exit code 2. With `--dry-run`, you can preview all replacements as a unified diff without writing changes to disk. With `--format json`, the command outputs structured migration data.


The First Fix
=============

Once your configuration is in place, run `dresscode fix` and commit the result in a dedicated commit without any logic changes. Even when rules appear to do "the exact same thing," automated fixes can differ in whitespace details from legacy formatters. Reviewing thousands of lines individually makes little sense; what matters is having a clean, isolated commit dedicated entirely to formatting. In large codebases where a single massive diff is impractical, clean up incrementally by set or section: run `dresscode fix --only cleanup` or `dresscode fix --only blankLines` and commit each step separately. A final full `fix` will then polish the rest (see [Cleaning Up Old Code Step by Step |configuration#Cleaning Up Old Code Step by Step]). Anything that `fix` cannot resolve automatically will remain in the terminal output. If there are more manual issues than you can address today, run `dresscode baseline` to record them in a [baseline |suppressions-and-baseline#baseline], allowing CI to pass immediately while reporting only newly introduced violations.


What Will Be Different
======================

A migration guide that promises a zero-friction transition loses your trust the moment you hit your first discrepancy. Here is an upfront breakdown of how things work differently:

- **Decisions instead of rules.** Your configuration does not list abstract rule classes and their options; it describes how your code should look: `blankLines.aroundMethods: 1` or `imports.unused: forbidden`. Which specific rules handle the execution is DressCode's business (see [Decisions Instead of Rules |how-it-works#Decisions Instead of Rules]). The complete catalogue is available on the [Rules |decisions/@home] page.
- **Priority numbers do not exist.** If your previous results depended on carefully orchestrating the execution order of fixers, you may see minor layout differences in some files. DressCode executes rules in passes until the AST reaches a stable steady state (see [No Priorities: Rules Run Until the Code Settles |how-it-works#No Priorities: Rules Run Until the Code Settles]).
- **Some rules have no counterpart.** Both `migrate config` and `migrate suppressions` will flag them explicitly. These are primarily granular documentation sniffs or rules that previously existed only to settle conflicts between other fixers.
- **Risky rules are not inherently risky here.** Fixers that PHP CS Fixer enabled only upon explicit request (because it could not distinguish safe from unsafe cases on a token stream) are resolved here using AST semantics, allowing safe occurrences to be fixed automatically. Edits that might alter runtime behavior are reported as a [risky fix |configuration#risky-fixes] and require permission via `fixRisky` or `--fix-risky`. Decisions that change behavior by their very nature (`expressions.comparison.loose: forbidden`, converting `==` to `===`) remain entirely under your explicit control.
- **Exit codes follow a standard scale**: `0` for clean code, `1` for actionable violations, `2` for file processing failures, and `3` for configuration or syntax errors. The full scale is detailed in the [Command Line Reference |cli#Exit Codes]. Because PHP_CodeSniffer and PHP CS Fixer use their own scales, CI scripts inspecting exit codes will need a minor adjustment.
- **One tool instead of two.** Many projects previously ran CodeSniffer for linting and Fixer for automated repairs, requiring two configurations and two CI stages. DressCode unifies both jobs under one engine.
- **Preview decisions without touching configuration.** Running `dresscode check --set controlFlow.elseAfterReturn=forbidden --only controlFlow.elseAfterReturn --diff` shows the exact diff a single decision would produce, and `dresscode fix --set …` applies it on top of your existing configuration, formatting changes according to your project standard (see [One Decision for One Run |configuration#One Decision for One Run: --set and --only]).
- **You configure intent rather than rule lists.** Configuration rests on two pillars: the standard defines visual appearance, and [sets |configuration#Standards and Sets] define what else you require from your code. There are exactly six curated sets, so adding `cleanup` to `use` will never mutate into twenty lines of rule names as the tool evolves.
- **Code modernization is built in.** DressCode not only formats your code, but also upgrades it to modern PHP syntax and updates third-party library APIs, all in the same pass and adhering to your chosen standard. You no longer need separate migration tools with independent configurations (see [Upgrading to a Newer PHP |upgrading-php] and [Upgrading Code |upgrade]).

Tool-specific guides: [PHP CS Fixer |from-php-cs-fixer], [PHP_CodeSniffer and Slevomat |from-phpcs], [Nette Coding Standard |from-nette-coding-standard], [Laravel Pint |from-laravel-pint].

